NASA climatic model: Venus was once convenient to live
According to the estimates of the new NASA climatic model, there were once conditions suitable for life on Venus.
Scientists at the NASA Goddard Space Studies Institute have developed a model to explore Venus's past by using similar tools and algorithms used by climatic scientists.
The model was designed to determine if Venus was home to conditions that were once similar to those on the Earth with its hot, water-free surface and a CO2-filled atmosphere.
Scientists in the past were explaining the low rotational speed of Venus with a thick layer of atmosphere.
However, the latest estimates suggest that Venus may have a thin, Earth-like atmosphere and can still rotate slowly.
The model also looked at old Venus' topography.
Although the planet has more dribbled territories than on Earth, it may have once been protected against sufficient water and rapid evaporation by geographical variability.
At the same time, the old Venus had benefited from a sleazy sun by 30 percent.
GiSS scientist Anthony Del Genio said in a news bulletin, "In the simulation of theGISS model, the slow rotation of Venus allowed the day side to the sun for almost two months at a time."
By warming the surface, it caused rains that form thick cloud layers that moved like an umbrella that protected the surface from sun-induced warming.
Consequently, only a few degrees climatic temperatures emerged, as the Earth was today.
The model created a story for Venus, similar to the evolution that planet scientists had previously propagated as hypotheses.
Although he once had suitable living conditions, Venus was subjected to a lot of heat and ultraviolet light over several billions of years.
They vaccinated the oceans and the water vapour was burned by ultraviolet.
Hydrogen's run out, and there's only a thick atmosphere full of carbon dioxide with a deadly greenhouse gas effect, Venus as we know it today.
The researchers shared the results of the new Venus climate model in Geophysical Research.
