Kepler measured the rotational speed of the stars in Ülker constellation
The Kepler space telescope helped astronomers to create the most complete catalog in terms of the rotational speeds of the stars in the Ülker constellant.
The new data helps astronomers to understand why the stars in the constellator rotate at a varied rate and their return periods are related to planetary formation.
Luisa Rebull, a research scientist at Caltech's Infrared Processing and Analystics Centre in Pasadena, said in a news bulletin, "while we compare our results with star clusters, we hope to learn more about the relationship between a star's mass, age, and even the history of the solar tract."
Ülker constellation is 445 light-years away from the world.
Most of the stars in the constellites were formed 125 million years ago, reaching a new level of new adults.
During their lifetime, they rotate at the highest possible speed.
At peak rotational rates, the stars at this maturation process form a large amount of star wind.
As these winds progress in the magnetic fields of the stars, there is a refractive effect on the stars.
As a star grows old, the rotational speed begins to drop.
Kepler measures the rotational speed of each star by following the time between the appearance of noticeable star spots such as sunspots.
The latest catalog of rotational speeds suggests that the larger stars rotate faster than small stars.
Rebull, comparing the stars to the dancers, stated that the big ones performed their return on one foot more slowly than the smaller ones.
Rebull said, "We can see those who turn slowly in the country's 'substitute' are much heavier and those who rotate fast are very light stars," Rebull said.
Since the Ülker constell be very close, it acts as an ideal cosmic laboratory for comparing star trajectories and rotational speeds.
Rebull said, "The country's constellation provides theoretical models for stellar rotations in both directions as young and old."
There's a lot we'd like to find out more about how the rotational speeds of the stars fall, when and why they've left their 'dance shoes' in a corner.
Rebull and his colleagues have detailed three new analyses of Ülker constellator's rotational speed in three new articles to be published in Astronomical Magazine in a short time.
